  "body": "## Change\n\nThe uninstaller Inno Setup embeds in the setup exe is now signed whenever a certificate is configured, along with the setup exe itself.\n\n- `scripts/sign.ps1` gains `-Status`, which signs nothing: exit 0 = certificate configured, 10 = not configured, 1 = half-configured. It is still the only script that reads `CAMCRAFT_SIGN_*` and the only place holding the signtool command and the timestamp URL.\n- `scripts/installer.ps1`: when `-Status` says configured, it passes `/DSignToolName=camcraft` plus `/Scamcraft=powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $q<sign.ps1>$q -Path $f` to ISCC. It then checks that the setup exe carries a signature. When `-Status` says not configured, it passes neither switch and logs `installer signing: off (no certificate configured)`; the ISCC command line is otherwise the same as before.\n- `scripts/installer/camcraft.iss`: `SignTool={#SignToolName}` + `SignedUninstaller=yes` sit under `#ifdef SignToolName`, so they are only on when installer.ps1 defines it.\n- `scripts/release.ps1` and `.github/workflows/release.yml`: removed the separate `sign.ps1` pass on the setup exe, because ISCC signs it now. The installer step in release.yml now has `CAMCRAFT_SIGN_CERT_PASSWORD` in its env. It was missing before, so with the secret set the old `sign.ps1` call there would have failed as half-configured. No triggers changed.\n- `scripts/README.md`: documents how the signing works and drops the \"uninstaller not signed yet\" note. The decision record is `docs/decisions/29-sign-uninstaller.md`.\n\n## Verified, and how\n\nISCC.exe (Inno Setup 6) is **not installed on this machine**, so a real Inno compile was **not run**. What I ran instead:\n\n- PowerShell parse check of sign.ps1, installer.ps1 and release.ps1 (0 errors), and a YAML parse of release.yml.\n- `sign.ps1 -Status` in each state: no env → exit 10; `CAMCRAFT_SIGN_CERT_PATH` alone → exit 1; thumbprint set → exit 0. `sign.ps1` with a thumbprint but no `-Path` → exit 1. With no env, `-Path x.exe` → exit 0, same as before.\n- Ran installer.ps1 end to end against a **stand-in ISCC.exe**: a throwaway console exe placed on PATH. It prints the arguments it receives, writes a setup exe and an `uninst.e32.tmp`, and, when `/Scamcraft=` is given, expands `$q`/`$f` the way Inno Setup documents and runs the result through cmd for each file:\n  - No certificate: no `/D`/`/S` reached ISCC and both files were NotSigned, so the unsigned path is unchanged.\n  - Self-signed code-signing cert (New-SelfSignedCertificate in CurrentUser\\My, removed afterwards and confirmed gone), `CAMCRAFT_SIGN_THUMBPRINT` set: the expanded command reached `sign.ps1`, and signtool signed `uninst.e32.tmp` with a DigiCert RFC-3161 timestamp (Get-AuthenticodeSignature showed the test cert as signer). `signtool verify /pa` then rejected the untrusted self-signed root, so sign.ps1 exited 1 and the build stopped with `INSTALLER FAILED: ISCC exited with 1`. That is the intended loud failure for a certificate that is configured but unusable. Because of that stop, the setup exe was never reached in this run. I did not trust the test root, since that needs an interactive Root-store prompt.\n- `release.ps1 -DryRun` stops at preflight on this branch: it needs a new version that matches RELEASE_NOTES.md. So only its parse check was run; the change there removes a post-ISCC sign call and nothing else.\n\n**Not exercised:** a real ISCC compile, with or without signing; a real installer whose `unins000.exe` is checked after a silent install; a trusted certificate. The first CI release with `CAMCRAFT_SIGN_CERT_BASE64` set, or a local `-Installer` cut with a real certificate, is where this gets exercised end to end. Check there that `dist/*-setup.exe` is signed, then run `setup.exe /VERYSILENT /DIR=<tmp>`, check `Get-AuthenticodeSignature <tmp>\\unins000.exe`, and uninstall with `unins000.exe /VERYSILENT`.\n\nPinning tests: none (build scripts only; no Rust test covers scripts/).\n\nChangelog: Windows installer: with a code-signing certificate configured (CAMCRAFT_SIGN_*), the embedded uninstaller is signed too, along with the setup exe\n\nFixes #29\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
